"Workflows for work that runs the business Mistral AI has released 'Workflows', an orchestration layer that supports stable operation of enterprise AI processes. Workflows provide durability, observability, and fault tolerance to ensure your AI models operate reliably in production. You can write workflows in Python and easily implement steps that require human intervention with a single line of code. Integration with Mistral Studio allows you to track and audit every execution. Introducing Forge Mistral AI announced 'Forge', an enterprise platform that helps companies build specialized AI models by learning their own internal data. Forge uses a company's internal documents, code base, operational records, etc. to build AI models optimized for domain knowledge. It supports pre-learning, post-learning, and reinforcement learning, and allows the development of agents that understand a company's specific workflow and internal policies. Leading organizations such as ASML, Ericsson, and the European Space Agency (ESA) are already using Forge to train AI for their systems. Error 500 (Server Error)!!1500.That’s an error.There was an error. Please try again later.That’s all we know. Mistral AI partners with NVIDIA to accelerate open frontier models Mistral AI announced a partnership with NVIDIA to jointly develop open frontier AI models. Mistral AI participates as a founding member of NVIDIA's 'NVIDIA Nemotron Coalition' and promotes the development of frontier-level open source AI models. We plan to combine Mistral AI's professional model architecture with NVIDIA's computing resources and data generation pipeline. As part of this collaboration, we have unveiled the 'Mistral Small 4' model to help developers and organizations innovate. Leanstral: Open-Source foundation for trustworthy vibe-coding Mistral AI has released ‘Leanstral’, an open source code agent designed to automate mathematical proofs and software specification verification. It is based on the Lean 4 proof aid and is optimized for formally proving mathematical objects and software logic. By adopting a sparse architecture using 6B active parameters, we achieved higher efficiency and performance compared to existing large models. We introduced FLTEval, a new benchmark to measure field-proven engineering capabilities, and demonstrated outperformance over many large models. Rails testing on autopilot: Building an agent that writes what developers won't A case in which the Mistral AI team developed an autonomous AI agent that increases test coverage and reduces debugging burden by automating the writing of test code in a large-scale Rails environment. Developed an autonomous test generation agent to solve the testing shortage problem caused by prioritizing feature development in Rails projects. The agent automatically creates and refines RSpec tests, adhering to style rules and coverage goals, and runs them within your CI/CD pipeline without human intervention. It is built based on Mistral's open source coding assistant, 'Vibe', and operates by utilizing repository-level context understanding, specialized technology, and custom tools. At Cannes Lions, NVIDIA Partners Reshape Advertising and Marketing With AI At Cannes Lions, NVIDIA, together with its partners, presented an example of revolutionizing autonomous operation and efficiency in the advertising and marketing industry through AI technology. Alembic leverages the NVIDIA DGX Vera Rubin system to scale Causal AI models that analyze the performance of marketing investments to enterprise scale. AWS utilizes NVIDIA Triton Inference Server to provide infrastructure that enables AI-based decision-making in the real-time advertising bidding process. With NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs and the cuEmbed library, Criteo nearly doubled its model training speed, saving approximately 17,000 GPU hours per year. Sync and Stream: GeForce NOW Connects to Members’ Game Libraries Across Devices NVIDIA's GeForce NOW supports cloud gaming so that users can synchronize their existing game libraries across various devices and enjoy them without interruption. GeForce NOW works with major PC game stores such as Steam, GOG, Ubisoft+, and EA apps, allowing users to play their games in the cloud. Using the cloud save function, you can synchronize your game progress between devices and continue playing on PC, mobile, Mac, TV, etc. This summer, GOG single sign-on and library sync features will be added to further improve user convenience. France Advances Europe’s AI Future With NVIDIA Technologies France is building AI infrastructure in Europe based on NVIDIA technology and large-scale investments and is leading the development of regionally specialized AI models. France is strengthening its AI infrastructure competitiveness through 'France 2030' and investment summits. Mistral has deployed 18,000 NVIDIA GB200 systems in France and aims to have 200 megawatts of computing capacity in Europe by 2027. The development of open AI models and datasets that reflect local language, culture, and European regulatory requirements is actively underway. Adobe embeds agentic AI workflows across Creative Cloud, shifting from media generation to production orchestration Adobe introduces 'Creative Agent' technology to automate and manage complex production workflows across Creative Cloud. It goes beyond simple media creation and acts as an orchestration layer that automates complex, multi-step production processes. Agents are integrated into major Adobe apps such as Premiere Pro, Photoshop, and Illustrator to perform repetitive tasks. Elements (visual variable library) and Projects (contextual memory layer) ensure consistency and continuity of work. We are pursuing integration with major third-party enterprise platforms such as ChatGPT, Claude, and Microsoft 365 Copilot. HSBC expands AI banking partnership with Google Cloud HSBC has entered into a multi-year partnership with Google Cloud to develop and deploy AI tools across its global operations. HSBC is working with Google Cloud and Google DeepMind to develop AI tools for asset management, financial crime risk management and internal decision support. Over the next two years, we expect to support more than 200 AI use cases, generating more than $100 million in increased revenue or efficiency for each initiative. We plan to actively introduce generative AI and agent AI to increase the speed of financial crime detection and improve the work and development efficiency of employees. Microsoft sells OpenAI models in China. OpenAI and Anthropic won’t. Microsoft has become the only U.S. AI model supplier by selling OpenAI models to Chinese companies through Azure, but conflict with model developers who are concerned about security and intellectual property rights issues continues. Microsoft is the only American company selling OpenAI models in the Chinese market, where OpenAI and Anthropic are reluctant to enter directly. Large Chinese companies such as ByteDance, Ant Group, Meituan, and Tencent are utilizing OpenAI models through Azure. OpenAI is concerned about model 'distilling' by Chinese customers, but Microsoft insists it is defending itself technically. The ‘godfather of AI’ called xAI a failure and warned of a bubble. He’s also selling the alternative. Yann LeCun defines Elon Musk's xAI as a failure, warns of the possibility of a bubble in the AI ​​industry, and promotes his own technology as an alternative. Yann LeCun evaluated xAI as a failed company, taking issue with the departure of xAI's core talent and Elon Musk's leadership style. xAI preserves profits by leasing computing resources to competitors to cover enormous infrastructure costs. LeCun warned that the AI ​​industry as a whole is in a 'bubble' state, relying on investor funds and failing to generate returns relative to actual costs. Through his startup AMI Labs, LeCun argues that a 'world model' approach is an alternative to the current large-scale language model. California expected a tax bonanza from AI IPOs. Modern compensation structures may blunt the windfall. It is said that the huge tax revenue effect that California expected from the IPO of AI companies may be less than expected due to the new compensation structure and tax saving strategy. Error 500 (Server Error)!!1500.That’s an error.There was an error. Please try again later.That’s all we know. New compensation and tax-saving strategies for technology company employees, such as single-term restricted stock (RSU), pre-listing stock sales, and stock-backed lending, are spreading out or reducing the tax generation on the day of listing. Unlike in the past, many employees sold stocks or used financial techniques even before listing, making the state's expected tax revenue unclear. An ex-Palantir leader raised $16M to point AI at the NHS’s paperwork, not its doctors Frontier Health, a British startup founded by a former Palantir employee, attracted $16 million in seed investment to develop ‘JUNO’, an AI agent to streamline administrative work in the NHS. While most AI for the NHS focuses on assisting medical staff, Frontier Health focuses on automating tasks for administrative teams. JUNO handles administrative tasks such as checking patient management routes, tracking test results, and rescheduling appointments, and can be deployed within 8 weeks without changing the existing IT system. In fact, the implementation of East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust has resulted in saving 221 staff days over eight weeks and reducing patient stay times in the emergency department by around 22%. As it is run by a former Palantir founder, it provides the operational value of Palantir, but is positioned as an alternative that is free from the political controversies Palantir is currently experiencing within the NHS. Baseten is raising $1.5bn at up to $13bn, betting AI’s profits lie in cheap inference Baseten is attracting $1.5 billion in investment, valuing the company at up to $13 billion with the goal of reducing AI model inference costs. Baseten provides inference software and computing capacity for companies to run AI models, primarily enabling cost-effective use of open source models. This investment is being made with a dual valuation structure of $11 billion and $13 billion, and the company's value has more than doubled in five months compared to $5 billion in January. Baseten's rapid growth is driven by the demand from enterprises to significantly reduce costs by shifting workloads to open source models. ByteDance is Microsoft’s biggest AI customer, even as Washington treats Chinese AI as a threat It covers the situation where ByteDance is continuing its AI business by purchasing Microsoft's cloud and open AI models on a large scale despite the AI ​​technology conflict between the United States and China. ByteDance is Microsoft's largest customer, spending more than $1 billion annually on Microsoft's AI and cloud services. OpenAI and Anthropic are prohibited from directly selling to China, but Microsoft is selling indirectly through Azure. ByteDance is utilizing Microsoft's model in preparation for U.S. sanctions, while simultaneously switching its hardware base to domestically produced AI chips. Pixi’s new iOS app turns text messages into interactive AR experiences Messaging app Pixi has launched an iOS app that combines AI and augmented reality (AR) to transform text messages into interactive AR characters. Via iMessage, you can send AI-powered AR characters that interact with their real-world surroundings and react in real time. All visual and audio data processing occurs internally on the device, protecting user privacy. In addition to the initial characters (robots, cats, etc.), we are planning a character marketplace involving third parties and user-customized character functions using generative AI in the future. How to turn off AI in your Google Docs We'll guide you through how to disable intrusive Gemini AI pop-ups and related smart features in Google Docs. You can turn off the AI ​​pop-up bar that appears at the bottom of the document through the 'Gemini' item in the top menu of Google Docs. Asking the Gemini chatbot how to delete does not actually disable any functionality; it simply closes the chat window. You can turn smart features on or off across your account in bulk from the 'Google Workspace Smart Features' management page within Gmail settings. Who decides when AI is too dangerous? An analysis of the confusion in the current AI regulatory environment through the incident where Anthropic's latest AI model, 'Fable 5', had its service interrupted due to the U.S. government's sudden export controls. The US government has imposed export controls restricting foreign access to Anthropic's new AI model 'Fable 5' and its base model 'Mythos'. Anthropic took these models completely offline due to lack of regulatory compliance capabilities. Anthropic, which has advocated the need for AI regulation, is perplexed by the government's actual method of applying regulations, and this incident raises questions about whether the US government's AI regulation policy is for safety or a political tool. Adobe’s redesigned AI studio remembers what your creations look like Adobe has announced new features for its Firefly AI assistant and a 'reimagined' AI Studio that can remember and reuse context for your creations. Firefly AI Studio remembers your creations to maintain consistent context, provide reusable assets and an organized workflow. The new 'Elements' feature lets you save and reuse characters, places and objects, saving you the hassle of entering prompts. The new 'Projects' feature allows you to continue your work by systematically managing your assets and creations. The Firefly AI assistant has added features such as brand logo creation, color palette construction, video clip assembly (Quick Cut), and storyboard visualization. Midjourney goes from generating cat images to full-body ultrasound scans Midjourney, an AI image generator company, announced the 'Midjourney Scanner', a full-body scanning medical device using ultrasound technology, and plans to establish a spa in San Francisco using it. ‘The Midjourney Scanner’, developed by Midjourney, is a full-body scanner that takes pictures of the inside of the human body through an ultrasonic sensor ring underwater. Developed in partnership with Butterfly Network, it creates 3D images of muscles, fat, bones, and organs in less than a minute. CEO David Holtz said the device would provide high-quality images similar to MRIs, and he hopes it could be used to measure routine body changes for preventive medicine purposes. Midjourny plans to open a 'Midjourny Spa' equipped with a gym, sauna, and hot and cold baths in San Francisco's Union Square by the end of 2027 and install this scanner. The KV Cache Compression Race: TurboQuant vs OSCAR vs EpiCache This article analyzes trends in KV cache compression technologies TurboQuant, OSCAR, and EpiCache to solve memory bottlenecks that occur when processing long contexts of large-scale language models (LLMs). The KV cache of LLM increases linearly with context length, occupying more memory than the model weight, and is the main cause of decoding speed. Technical approaches for KV cache compression are divided into token deletion, quantization, low-rank projection, merging, and architecture sharing. TurboQuant uses a data-independent, theoretically optimized quantization scheme to achieve virtually lossless performance at the 3-4 bit level. OpenAI Releases LifeSciBench, a 750-Task Benchmark Grading AI Models on Real Life-Science Research With Expert-Written Rubric OpenAI has released 'LifeSciBench', a benchmark comprised of 750 expert-written tasks to evaluate complex research challenges in the life sciences field. LifeSciBench consists of 750 subjective tasks written by experts to evaluate practical decision-making processes in life science research. It covers seven workflows and seven biological domains, with approximately 79% of tasks requiring multi-step inference. Tasks were written by 173 PhD-level experts and accurately evaluated using a rubric containing 19,020 evaluation criteria. As a result of the benchmark, even the top model, GPT-Rosalind, showed a low task passing rate of about 36.1%, suggesting the need to improve the performance of AI models in the life sciences field. NVIDIA SkillSpector Guide: Scanning AI Skills for Security Risks with Static Analysis and SARIF Reports This guide explains how to proactively detect and analyze security risks in AI skills using NVIDIA SkillSpector. It provides a systematic way to assess the security risks of AI skills before applying them to real-world workflows. Detect vulnerable AI skills through static analysis and SkillSpector's LangGraph workflow. Detection results can be organized and visualized in pandas, exported in SARIF format, or custom analyzers and LLM-based semantic analysis can be added. Advanced Join Techniques: LATERAL Joins, Semi Joins, Anti Joins Explains the concept and use of LATERAL, Semi, and Anti joins, which are advanced join techniques to solve complex problems when processing SQL data. LATERAL joins allow subqueries in the FROM clause to refer to columns of the previous table, which is useful when applying a set-returning function on a row-by-row basis. Semi join returns rows from the left table without duplicates if there is a matching row in the right table. Anti joins return data only if there are no matching rows in the right table. NAVI-Orbital: First In-Orbit Demonstration of a Zero-Shot Vision-Language Model for Autonomous Earth Observation We developed the 'NAVI-Orbital' system, which analyzes and processes data in real time by directly running a visual-language model on an Earth observation satellite. The first successful autonomous multimodal inference using a visual-language model (Gemma 3) in a low-Earth orbit satellite. Solve bandwidth limitations by performing semantic compression within the satellite instead of traditional data transmission methods. Satellite control and rework are possible through natural language conversation, and agents cooperate based on LangGraph. CaVe-VLM-CoT: An Interpretable Vision-Language Model Framework The CaVe-VLM-CoT framework introduces a 5-stage recursive pipeline that forces evidence-based reasoning to reduce hallucination phenomena and a new evaluation metric, CaVeScore. To solve the hallucination problem of existing VLM, we propose CaVe-VLM-CoT, which has a five-step cyclical pipeline of extraction, search, resolution, citation injection, and verification. In the verification stage, if an unfounded claim is detected, a structure is introduced that sends feedback to the extractor and performs a re-search. We propose 23 component-specific indicators that measure search quality, step-by-step citation reliability, and cross-modal evidence, as well as an integrated indicator, CaVeScore. CEO-Bench: Can Agents Play the Long Game? This study introduces the 'CEO-Bench' benchmark to evaluate the long-term, complex task performance of AI models. We point out that language model agents are good at short-term tasks, but have limitations at performing complex long-term strategies. CEO-Bench evaluates agents' abilities through a simulation of running a hypothetical startup for 500 days and managing marketing, budget, pricing, etc. Most of the latest models are struggling in this environment, with only Claude Opus 4.8 and GPT-5.5 exceeding $1M in starting capital but struggling to generate sustained profits. DeFAb: A Verifiable Benchmark for Defeasible Abduction in Foundation Models Analysis of the structure and limitations of LLM of a new benchmark dataset 'DeFAb' designed to evaluate the logical hypothesis reasoning ability of the basic model. DeFAb is a large-scale dataset designed to logically infer and verify hypotheses based on a public knowledge base accumulated over decades. While existing rule-based logic solvers achieve 100% accuracy, state-of-the-art language models record up to 23.5% accuracy under robust evaluation conditions, revealing a lack of logical reasoning performance. This dataset provides over 370,000 instances and is a rigorous measure of the model's ability to construct theoretically correct logical corrections, rather than simply generating fluent sentences. Optimizing Lithium Production Decisions under Geological, Demand, and Pricing Uncertainties: A POMDP Framework for Multi-Objective Decision Making This study proposes an optimal decision-making model using the POMDP framework to manage geological, price, and demand uncertainties that arise during the lithium production process. Designed a framework based on Partially Observed Markov Decision Process (POMDP) ​​to address the complexity of lithium mining decisions. Determine optimal mining timing and production methods by considering price, demand, geological variability and various extraction techniques. Shows better decision-making performance than existing human intuition-based heuristic models. Gaussian Mixture Attention: Linear-Time Sequence Mixing via Probabilistic Latent Routing In order to solve the long context processing bottleneck of transformer architecture, this study proposed 'Gaussian Mixture Attention (GMA)', an attention mechanism of linear time complexity using Gaussian mixture components. Instead of traditional pairwise token comparisons, GMA routes data through K learned Gaussian mixture components. The existing O(N^2) memory complexity has been reduced to O(NK), increasing efficiency in long context processing. It has shown competitive performance with existing attention mechanisms in long-context classification tasks and presents a probabilistic and interpretable alternative. CODEBLOCK: Learning to Supervise Code at the Right Granularity To increase the learning efficiency of code LLM, we propose the 'CodeBlock' framework, which learns in units of structurally meaningful code blocks rather than entire tokens. Traditional full token-based map refinement (SFT) is inefficient as it assumes all tokens are equally important. CodeBlock improves data efficiency by selecting and learning only important code blocks, considering the structural completeness and dependability of the code. As a result of six code generation benchmark experiments, it showed higher performance than the full token learning method while significantly reducing the number of tokens subject to supervised learning to 1.9%. Artemis: Anatomy-Resolved inTervention for Eliminating Multimodal NeuroImage confounderS We propose Artemis, a domain-specific causal framework for effectively removing demographic confounding factors in multimodal neuroimaging analysis. Existing graph neural networks (GNNs) have the problem of making incorrect predictions by confusing demographic factors such as age and gender with actual brain connectivity. Artemis performs causal interventions for each brain region, tuning it to avoid learning biases about specific demographic factors. Through three benchmark experiments, including ADNI, OASIS, and HCP, performance improvement and neuroscientific interpretation potential were demonstrated compared to existing GNN models. A Link between Shock-wave Theory and Symmetry-reduced Stochastic Gradient Descent for Artificial Neural Networks A study analyzing the dynamics of stochastic gradient descent that occurs during the learning process of an artificial neural network by mathematically linking it with shock wave theory. By removing the parameter symmetry of the neural network and applying local entropy roughness, we demonstrate that the effective learning dynamics satisfies the viscous Hamilton-Jacobi equation. In a space where symmetry is removed, the slope of the loss function follows the Burgers-type equation, which strictly explains the formation of shock waves during the neural network learning process. It can be applied to various architectures such as multi-layer perceptron, CNN, and Transformer, and provides a theoretical foundation for monitoring and controlling learning phase transitions. Attribution-Guided and Coverage-Maximized Pruning for Structural MoE Compression This study proposes a detailed structural pruning technique at the channel level rather than at the expert level to reduce the memory occupancy and inference cost of the mixed expert (MoE) model. Existing expert-level pruning has limitations in compression performance because it cannot capture subtle redundancies within the model. Considering that information within MoE experts is concentrated in some channels, we developed a channel-level structural pruning framework. The pruning rate allocation problem is reformulated into a channel score coverage maximization problem and efficiently solved through attribute-based approximation. Continuous Audio Thinking for Large Audio Language Models This study proposes a continuous latent action space (CoAT) framework using expert distillation to enable Large Audio Language Models (LALMs) to retain acoustic information lost during response generation. LALM is trained mainly for text generation, so it has the limitation of losing rich acoustic information such as voice details, prosody, and acoustic events. CoAT processes information distilled from expert models into a continuous latent space to organize acoustic information before generating a response. Demonstrated performance improvement in Qwen2-Audio, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B, Audio Flamingo 3, etc. with single prefill processing without additional autoregressive decoding cost. Redact or Keep? A Fully Local AI Cascade for Educational Dialogue De-Identification We propose a fully localized AI de-identification framework to preserve learning content while protecting privacy in educational conversation data. We propose a local AI cascade framework to resolve the trade-off between data security and accuracy faced by existing personal information de-identification methods. Effectively prevents the problem of misidentifying and deleting educational jargon through a two-stage structure of candidate generation (proposer) and situational awareness review (reviewer). It can be run in a single laptop environment and records a Macro F1 performance of 0.958, which is higher than commercial API and existing LLM-based methods. SproutRAG: Attention-Guided Tree Search with Progressive Embeddings for Long-Document RAG A study on SproutRAG, a new framework that enables hierarchical search without additional LLM calls in long document-based RAGs. A hierarchical RAG framework that uses learned inter-sentence attention to organize the document structure into a binary tree. Supports multiple granularity searches without traditional LLM calls or lossy summarization methods. In benchmarks in various fields such as science and law, information efficiency (IE) has improved by an average of 6.1% compared to the existing highest level. Want Better Synthetic Data? Steer It: Activation Steering for Low-Resource Language Generation A study using activation steering techniques to streamline synthetic data generation for low-resource languages. Instead of the existing few-shot prompting method, 'language steering' targeting the identity of the language and 'quality steering' strategy to control quality were introduced. Research shows that steering in early layers increases the diversity of generated data and improves downstream task performance. We confirmed that the steering technique is effective in generating low-resource language data and learning small-scale classifiers. JetFlow: Breaking the Scaling Ceiling of Speculative Decoding with Parallel Tree Drafting JetFlow is a new Speculative Decoding framework that dramatically improves the inference speed of large language models (LLMs) by optimizing the parallel tree drafting method. JetFlow breaks through the performance scalability limitations of traditional Speculative Decoding by combining head-based efficiency and causal parallel drafting. We achieve longer acceptance lengths and improved speed by leveraging the hidden state of the target model to generate candidate trees aligned with the model's autoregressive factors. We consistently demonstrate superior performance compared to existing interactive head- and tree-based SD baselines in math, coding, and conversation benchmarks. Beyond LoRA: Can you beat the most popular fine-tuning technique? We cover an overview of PEFT (Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning) technology for memory-efficient fine-tuning of open source models, as well as the status and alternatives to the overwhelmingly used LoRA technique. To address the high memory requirements of fine tuning, Parameter Efficient Fine Tuning (PEFT) technology was developed. LoRA is the most popular PEFT technique that saves memory by fixing the base model and learning only a small number of parameters. Depending on the use case, LoRA may not always be the best choice, so various PEFT alternatives may need to be examined. Show GN: htmlbook – HTML created by AI, shared with a single link rather than sent as a file Introducing htmlbook, a service that allows you to easily share AI-generated HTML or Markdown documents with a single link without file transfer. Reports or documents created by AI can be viewed immediately by sharing a link without downloading or taking screenshots. You can access it on browsers and mobile devices by creating a public URL through MCP integration or file upload. Provides theme (paper/sepia/dark) support, table of contents creation, and markdown original preservation functions. Anthropic officially opens Seoul office... All-round partnership with Naver, Nexon, Samsung SDS, LG CNS, etc. Anthropic officially opened its Seoul office and entered into full-fledged entry into the Korean market by signing comprehensive partnerships with major domestic companies. We officially opened our Seoul office as our third base in the Asia-Pacific region following Tokyo and Bengaluru. We have formed partnerships with Korean companies in various fields, including Naver, Nexon, LG CNS, and Samsung SDS. The fact that the per capita usage of Claude in Korea is 3.5 times the world average is the main reason for entering the Korean market. Entrepreneur’s Playbook: Creating an AI-native Startup Anthropic has released the ‘Entrepreneur Playbook’, a practical guide to building a startup using AI, from idea verification to expansion. We divide the startup life cycle into four stages: Idea, MVP, Launch, and Scale, and present an AI-based workflow for each stage. Learn how to transform the role of entrepreneurs from practitioners to orchestrators to increase efficiency and automate repetitive tasks. Some criticism points out that the material is focused on promoting Anthropic tools rather than developing actual AI native products. OpenAI's losses increase nearly 8x in 2025... $34 billion spent OpenAI's 2025 financial report shows net losses increasing sharply compared to the previous year, raising concerns about profitability and sustainability. OpenAI's net loss attributable to the company in 2025 will be $38.53 billion, approximately 8 times higher than in 2024. Huge losses were reflected due to changes in the fair value of liabilities incurred in the process of converting from a non-profit to a for-profit corporation. Sales recorded $13.07 billion, but total expenses, including costs paid to Microsoft and others, reached $34 billion, resulting in large operating losses. US suspends all blacklisting of DeepSeek and over 100 companies classified as security risks Addresses a situation where the U.S. government is intentionally withholding the trade blacklisting of over 100 Chinese companies, including DeepSeek and CXMT, amid the Trump administration's policy of easing tensions with China. The U.S. Department of Commerce is not placing more than 100 Chinese companies on a trade blacklist despite national security risks, as approved by a joint interagency committee. The holdup creates the longest gap in new listings since October last year, raising concerns that U.S. technology could flow into Chinese military and intelligence operations. DeepSeek is included in the blacklist for reasons such as military and intelligence support, illegal attempts to extract US AI model capabilities, and use of restricted Nvidia chips. What does provisional paper acceptance mean in ECCV? Is that the default message everyone gets? [D] This is a question about the meaning of 'provisional acceptance' of a paper at the ECCV conference and whether this is the basic message sent to everyone. We are asking about the meaning of the notification of paper review results from the European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV). We would like to ensure that this notification is sent universally to all submitters. Is ACL now irrelevant? [D] This article discusses whether the status of the ACL conference in the academic community has declined compared to the past, or whether it is still valid for applications to doctoral programs. Questioning the opinion that ACL is not a strength when applying to doctoral programs ACL is considered A+ rated and compared to other major AI conferences (NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, CVPR) Discussion of the differences between traditional computer science disciplines and AI conferences HELP WITH RESEARCH: Observation - Semantically Dense Context Produces Strong Late-Layer Divergence Without Jailbreak Prompts [D] A request for researchers to observe and verify the possibility that long, semantically dense, generic text can influence the latent space of an AI model to circumvent alignment constraints. The model's internal state and output results are changed using only semantically dense text, without specific commands or jailbreak prompts. This phenomenon is likely to bypass existing political and ethical guardrails and generate more critical responses than usual. Researchers are tracking changes in the internal activation layer and attention weight of the open source model and seeking expert verification as to whether this phenomenon is an artifact or real. Any idea if AAAI will be harsh on computer vision paper as last year? [R] A researcher planning to submit a computer vision-related paper to the AAAI conference inquires about the possibility of replicating last year's low acceptance rate. A researcher considering submitting a paper in the field of computer vision is concerned about the low passing rate of AAAI last year. It is known that last year, AAAI issued instructions to reviewers to adjust the acceptance rate in the computer vision field lower than in other fields. We are seeking community input on whether a similar policy will be applied this year or whether it will return to previous years. GLM-5.2 Flash when? (joke) Comments from users expressing joy over Z.ai's open-sourcing of GLM 5.2 and anticipating the release of the 'Flash' version, the successor to GLM-4.7-flash. Users responded positively to Z.ai's open source of GLM 5.2. Users hope for the release of a new 'Flash' model, the successor to GLM-4.7-flash. The desired model size is between 27B and 120B parameters (MoE or Dense method). Leaked financial docs show OpenAI is losing billions of dollars a year Leaked financial documents show that OpenAI is losing billions of dollars every year. Documents related to OpenAI's financial situation have been leaked. According to the document, OpenAI is losing billions of dollars every year. This issue is becoming a hot topic in IT communities such as Reddit. Kwai-Keye/Keye-VL-2.0-30B-A3B-GGUF · Hugging Face News of the launch of 'Keye-VL-2.0-30B-A3B', a 30B class multimodal model with enhanced long video understanding and agent functions. It shows performance equal to or better than Gemini-3-Flash in terms of long image understanding and temporal location ability. Long context architecture based on DSA (DeepSeek Sparse Attention) supports efficient computation and 256K context processing. It is the first to be equipped with an agent collaboration mechanism including code, tools, and web search capabilities to perform practical tasks. the power of intelligence is better in the hands of the people than in the board rooms of tycoons. PearlOS, an open source project, has unveiled the 'Agency' function, which automatically selects the optimal model for each task by combining various local and cloud LLMs in the form of swarm intelligence. PearlOS was selected for the NVIDIA Inception Program and aims to develop a lightweight AI model capable of multimodal processing. The new 'Agency' function selects and utilizes the best model among various models such as DeepSeek, Kimi, and Qwen in real time depending on the characteristics of the task. When performance of a specific model deteriorates, it automatically switches to a superior model immediately, providing users with a consistent, high-performance AI experience. gave my local llm agent mcp tools for local image + video gen, so it just generates when i ask (fully offline+free) Implemented the ability for local LLM agents to directly create images and videos offline and for free using MCP tools. Local LLM agent utilizes Model Context Protocol (MCP) tool Image and video creation works completely offline and for free The agent directly performs the creation task according to the user's request. I put Siri AI through the same tests I use for ChatGPT and Gemini on MacOS 27 - here's how it did This is an analysis article comparing the performance of the new Apple Intelligence-based Siri with the existing ChatGPT and Gemini in the same testing environment. The new Siri is more useful than the old Siri, but it still makes mistakes and needs improvement in accuracy and conversation flow. Apple Intelligence features require the latest OS version, such as macOS 27, and a device that supports it. Users must install the developer beta and join the waitlist to try the new Siri. I found the best SSD and storage deals ahead of Amazon Prime Day - including Lexar and Seagate Ahead of the upcoming Amazon Prime Day, we introduce discount information on SSDs and storage from major brands such as Lexar and Seagate. Ahead of the Amazon Prime Day sale, various premium M.2 and external SSD products are on sale. This is an opportunity to purchase a high-performance SSD for business or gaming PC upgrades at a low price. Products from trusted brands such as Lexar and Seagate, Samsung, and Western Digital are recommended. Google Home Speaker (2026) vs. Amazon Echo Dot Max: Why I'm split on these $99 rivals This article compares and analyzes the main features and performance of the newly released Google Home Speaker and Amazon Echo Dot Max. Google Home Speaker will be released on June 25th for $99 and will be equipped with ‘Gemini for Home’, a generative AI. The Amazon Echo Dot Max features Alexa+ and is currently on sale for $65. Both products are competing fiercely as smart speakers that leverage generative AI to perform complex tasks. Linux users face a Microsoft Secure Boot headache - here's the painkiller This article discusses the impact of Microsoft's Secure Boot certificate expiration on Linux users and how to respond. Microsoft's Secure Boot certificate has an expiration date, causing Linux users to worry about future boot issues. Most major Linux distributions have maintained compatibility with the Secure Boot system by using the Microsoft-signed 'shim' bootloader. Although certificate expiration does not mean that the system will be immediately unbootable, it does highlight the need for the Linux community to reduce external dependencies and establish its own secure boot management system. I discovered my ideal Android Auto setup by tweaking these 4 default settings We'll cover four Android Auto preferences you should change to be safer and reduce distractions while driving. Android Auto's default settings are complex and can become an unnecessary distraction while driving, requiring customization. You can disable the 'Start music automatically' feature to prevent the last media you were listening to from automatically playing when the car starts. You can adjust message preview, group conversation notification, and message notification sound settings to prevent navigation screen occlusion and reduce unnecessary notification disruption. Presentation: Write-Ahead Intent Log: A Foundation for Efficient CDC at Scale DoorDash engineers describe the 'Write-Ahead Intent Log (WAIL)' architecture developed to overcome the limitations of existing methods for efficient data change capture (CDC) for large-scale order processing. Existing CDC methods face performance limitations in high traffic environments during peak hours. Write-Ahead Intent Log (WAIL) improves processing efficiency by separating data change intent and status payload. Simplifies architecture and ensures scalability using dumb producer proxy and smart consumer patterns. Microsoft Scout, New  Enterprise Autopilot Built on OpenClaw, Announced at Build 2026 Microsoft announced 'Microsoft Scout', a next-generation agent that performs tasks autonomously, at Build 2026. Microsoft Scout is an always-on agent in the 'autopilot' category that works autonomously on your behalf without you having to ask for it every time. Based on OpenClaw, an open source agent framework, it can perform high-privilege tasks such as working with local files, running shell scripts, and automated browser session management. To enhance security within the enterprise, we assigned unique Entra IDs to individual instances and applied Microsoft Purview and DLP policies to mitigate risk. The Scripts on Your Checkout Page Are Now a PCI DSS Problem About the security threats posed by numerous third-party scripts running on payment pages and the need for new compliance with PCI DSS v4.0.1. Modern checkout pages load dozens of third-party scripts, which can lead to web skimming attacks. Requirements 6.4.3 and 11.6.1 of PCI DSS v4.0.1 mandate script management and tampering detection on payment pages. Reflectiz helps you efficiently comply with these PCI DSS regulations by monitoring the behavior of your scripts. DragonForce Hackers Abuse Microsoft Teams Relays to Hide Backdoor.Turn C2 Traffic DragonForce ransomware attackers have deployed new malware 'Backdoor.Turn' that exploits Microsoft Teams' TURN relay infrastructure to conceal C2 communications. The attackers exploited the legitimate TURN relay in Microsoft Teams to disguise C2 traffic as regular traffic. The Backdoor.Turn malware goes through the Teams infrastructure and connects to the real attacker's C2 server via a QUIC session. The attack used BYOVD techniques, leveraging DLL sideloading and vulnerable drivers, to defeat security software and gain persistent network access. Antropic upgrades ‘Claude Design’...Evolves into an enterprise platform Antropic has made a large-scale update to its AI design tool 'Clauded Design', evolving it into an enterprise platform that directly connects the company's design system and code environment. The design system import function allows AI to automatically learn and comply with the company's brand guidelines and design rules. Supports two-way integration with closed code, solving handoff issues between design and development and producing consistent results. By increasing token consumption efficiency and adding export capabilities to various external platforms, we have positioned ourselves as a hub for design work. 3B model surpasses Gemini 3 Pro's math skills... Weibo sparks benchmark debate The ultra-small AI model 'VibeSinker-3B' developed by China's Weibo researchers recorded mathematical and coding inference performance that surpassed that of large models, sparking a performance verification debate in the AI ​​industry. 'VibeSinker-3B' with 3 billion parameters recorded performance exceeding that of large models such as Gemini 3 Pro in math and coding benchmarks. The researchers proposed the 'parameter compression-coverage hypothesis', which states that the inference ability that can verify correct answers can be efficiently compressed even with a small number of parameters. Some have raised suspicions about 'bench maxing', which focuses only on improving scores, and have argued for the need to verify actual ability. Nvidia unveils 'autonomous research' agent robot... Taught itself to assemble GPU NVIDIA has unveiled 'ENPIRE', an 'autonomous research' agent technology that allows robots to perform complex physical tasks by experimenting and learning on their own. ENPIRE is a framework in which robots learn new tasks on their own through trial and error in a real environment with minimal human intervention. Multiple AI coding agents utilize robot swarms and resources to learn papers and collaborate with each other to solve difficult, precise tasks. As a result of the experiment, the problem-solving speed was greatly improved through the parallel work of the robot swarm, and detailed manipulation capabilities, such as installing a graphics card, were demonstrated. “Misos will not be released”... The first person to warn was a 35-year-old Antropic researcher. We cover the case and background of researcher Nicolas Carlini, who first warned of the serious cybersecurity vulnerability risk of Antropic's AI model 'Misos'. Nicolas Carlini, an Antropic researcher and hacker, opposes the general release of the AI ​​model 'Missos' and strongly warns of security risks. Carlini confirmed that 'Misos' quickly discovered hundreds of zero-day vulnerabilities in Linux and Ghost software through his own technique, 'Carlini Loop'. Currently, Carlini is in charge of persuading senior White House officials and discussing ways to control the risks of AI and its safe official launch. Open AI introduces ‘distribution simulation’ before model release… “Detecting side effects in advance” To evaluate the safety of next-generation AI models, OpenAI introduced 'deployment simulation' technology that verifies the model in a virtual service environment similar to the actual user environment. To complement the limitations of existing red team evaluations, we have unveiled a deployment simulation technique that uses actual user conversation records to predict the model's response in advance. This method more accurately predicts the risk incidence after actual deployment through the advantages of expanding the evaluation scope, reducing selection bias, and alleviating the model's 'evaluation recognition' problem. It maintains a high level of realism not only in simple chatbots but also in complex tool usage environments such as coding agents, and is effective in detecting alignment problems. Foxconn demonstrates Europe's first humanoid robot... Robot training is possible in just a few hours At VivaTech 2026 in Paris, Foxconn demonstrated for the first time in Europe a humanoid robot that can be trained in a few hours based on NVIDIA technology. Foxconn is promoting the commercialization of humanoid robots through the 'Physical AI Platform' that vertically integrates computing, robots, and factories. Using NVIDIA's Verarubin NVL72 and Isaac GR00T, we introduced a method of learning in a virtual environment and then fine-tuning it in the field. We have secured competitiveness in mass production by shortening robot training time from several months to several hours. “A robot at a standstill in a spectacular demonstration”… SW/manufacturing dualization is a bottleneck Minpyo Kim, CEO of Doosan Robotics, emphasized the importance of close cooperation and data integration between hardware manufacturers and AI software companies for the growth of the robot industry. There is a technological gap between the spectacular demonstration of the robot and its actual industrial application due to the failure of hardware and software to link together. Robotic automation is essential due to the shortage of manpower in 